  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Monday)\nIssued at 1210 AM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nUnseasonably warm temperatures are forecast across West Central \nTexas on Wednesday. Highs will be in the upper 70s to mid 80s. \nOvernight lows will be in the upper 50s to mid 60s. \n\nOn Thursday, a large upper level trough will be located across the \nwestern CONUS, placing West Central Texas in southwest flow aloft. A \ndisturbance is forecast to track northeast across West Texas late\nThursday into Friday. Ahead of this feature, moisture (mid and \nupper level) from the remnants of Tropical Storm Vance are \nforecast to move northeast into the region during this time frame.\nThis will result in an increase in showers and thunderstorms \nacross the area. Medium to high (40-80%) rain chances are forecast\non Thursday, with medium to high (60-100%) rain chances Thursday \nnight into Friday morning. The highest rain chances will be across\nthe northern Edwards Plateau and Concho Valley. Precipitable \nwater values (PWAT) will increase to 1.6 to 1.9 inches. These PWAT\nvalues are anomalously high for this time of the year, so locally\nheavy rainfall is possible. The main concern will be the \npotential for flooding due to heavy rainfall. Rain chances on \nFriday will steadily shift north, with much drier conditions \nreturning by late in the day. Highs on Thursday will be in the mid\n70s to lower 80s. Highs on Friday will be in the low to mid 70s. \n\nLow to medium (20-40%) rain chances are forecast on Saturday \n(Halloween), mainly during the morning and afternoon hours. Drier \nconditiions are forecast by Saturday evening. Highs on Saturday \nwill be in the mid to upper 70s.",
